---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import { site } from "../../site.config";

/*
 * The web copy of what the app's own "About & policies" screen says
 * (mobile/lib/core/screens/policy_screen.dart, strings policyRole* /
 * policyComplaints* / policyPayments* in app_en.arb). The three middle sections
 * are deliberately kept word-for-word in step with those strings: the whole
 * point of the screen is that the answer was on record beforehand rather than
 * invented during a dispute, and two versions saying slightly different things
 * would destroy that. If the ARB strings change, change this page too.
 */
const updated = "2 September 2026";
---
<Base
  brand="supplyo"
  title="SupplyO — Terms of Use"
  description="What SupplyO is and is not answerable for: the supplier is the seller, complaints about goods go to him, and payments are his to collect."
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-bold text-brand">SupplyO Terms of Use</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 font-mono text-sm text-ink-soft">Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <Prose>
      <p>
        These terms apply to everyone using SupplyO, whether you are a supplier
        running a delivery business or a customer ordering from one. They are the
        same words shown inside the app under <strong>About &amp; policies</strong>.
        By using SupplyO you accept them.
      </p>

      <h2>What SupplyO does</h2>
      <p>
        SupplyO is operated by {site.legalName}. It records orders, deliveries and
        bills. It does not sell, pack or deliver anything. The supplier you order
        from is the seller — the goods, their quality, weight and price are his,
        and your agreement is with him.
      </p>

      <h2>Complaints about goods</h2>
      <p>
        If something delivered is wrong — short weight, poor quality, the wrong
        item, or a price you did not agree — speak to your supplier directly. His
        number is on his profile under My suppliers. SupplyO cannot inspect goods,
        and does not settle disputes, replace items or issue refunds.
      </p>

      <h2>Payments</h2>
      <p>
        You pay the supplier, never SupplyO. The app only records the payments your
        supplier enters, so a bill here is his account of what is owed, not a demand
        from us. If a payment you made is missing from your bill, tell your supplier.
      </p>

      <h2>Your account</h2>
      <p>
        Your phone number identifies you and is how you sign in. Keep your password
        to yourself — anyone who has it can place orders in your name. Tell us at
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> if you think someone else
        has got into your account.
      </p>
      <p>
        Do not use SupplyO to place orders you do not intend to accept, to enter
        information about people who have not agreed to it, or to attempt to reach
        another supplier's or customer's records. We may suspend an account that does.
      </p>

      <h3>If you are a supplier</h3>
      <p>
        You are the seller. The rates you publish, the goods you deliver and the
        bills you raise are yours, as is your responsibility to the customer and for
        your own tax and licensing obligations. You see personal information about
        your customers in order to serve them; use it for that and nothing else, and
        see the <a href="/supplyo/privacy">privacy policy</a> for what that means.
      </p>

      <h2>Availability</h2>
      <p>
        SupplyO is provided as it is. We try to keep it running and accurate, but we
        cannot promise it will always be available or free of faults, and it is not a
        substitute for your own records. Nothing here limits any right you have under
        Pakistani law that cannot be excluded.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        We may update these terms. Significant changes will be shown in the app and
        the date above will change.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        {site.legalName}
        {site.address && <>, {site.address}</>}.
        <br />
        <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </div>
</Base>
